### 4. patch-agent-canvas-working-profile.ps1

#### 4.1 No Backup Before Modification
**Severity:** High  
**Location:** `patch-agent-canvas-working-profile.ps1`, Lines 15-25

**Failure Mechanism:**
```powershell
# Line 15-20: Modifies index.html without creating backup first
$Content = [System.IO.File]::ReadAllText($IndexHtml, [System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8)
```
If the patch fails, there's no way to restore the original file.

**Refactored Code:**
```powershell
# Create backup before any modification
$IndexBackup = Join-Path $CanvasDir 'index.html.backup-working-profile'
if (-not (Test-Path $IndexBackup)) {
    Copy-Item $IndexHtml $IndexBackup -Force
    Write-Host "[Working Profile UI] Created backup: $IndexBackup" -ForegroundColor Gray
}

# Verify backup integrity
$originalHash = (Get-FileHash $IndexHtml).Hash
$backupHash = (Get-FileHash $IndexBackup).Hash
if ($originalHash -ne $backupHash) {
    Write-Error "Backup integrity check failed"
    exit 1
}
```

#### 4.2 Regex Injection Vulnerability
**Severity:** High  
**Location:** `patch-agent-canvas-working-profile.ps1`, Lines 20-25

**Failure Mechanism:**
```powershell
# Line 20: Regex replacement without proper escaping
$Content = $Content -replace '<script id="oh-wp-loader">[\s\S]*?</script>', ''
```
If index.html contains malicious regex patterns, this could cause unexpected behavior.

**Refactored Code:**
```powershell
# Use literal string matching with proper escaping
$loaderPattern = [regex]::Escape('<script id="oh-wp-loader">') + '[\s\S]*?' + [regex]::Escape('</script>')
$Content = $Content -replace $loaderPattern, ''
```

